      "side": 1,
      "message": "Another acceptable return value might be 400 Bad Request, since it\u0027s a validation thing.\n\nI guess the following is how I think about it.\n\n  ref \u003d {\n      trustor_user_id: $TRUSTOR_USER_ID,\n      trustee_user_id: $TRUSTEE_USER_ID,\n      project_id: $PROJECT_ID,\n      roles: $MY_ROLE,\n      random: \u0027garbage\u0027\n  }\n\nShould return a 400 Bad Request because `random` isn\u0027t an attribute we accept to for trusts. Today this should return a 500.\n\n  ref \u003d {\n      trustor_user_id: $TRUSTOR_USER_ID,\n      trustee_user_id: $TRUSTEE_USER_ID,\n      project_id: $PROJECT_ID,\n      random: \u0027garbage\u0027\n  }\n\nShould return a 403 since `roles` are a required attribute of the request but they aren\u0027t included. Which should be the same outcome as:\n\n  ref \u003d {\n      trustor_user_id: $TRUSTOR_USER_ID,\n      trustee_user_id: $TRUSTEE_USER_ID,\n      project_id: $PROJECT_ID,\n  }\n\nWe could showcase this with a couple different test cases, one of which will likely be wip\u0027d.\n\n  def test_create_trust_with_extra_attributes_fails(self):\n      ref \u003d unit.new_trust_ref(trustor_user_id\u003dself.user_id,\n                               trustee_user_id\u003dself.trustee_user_id,\n                               project_id\u003dself.project_id,\n                               roles\u003dself.role_id)\n      ref.update({\u0027roles\u0027: {\u0027fake_key\u0027: \u0027fake_value\u0027}})\n\n      # Should return 400 Bad Request because `fake_key` is an extra\n      # attribute that keystone doesn\u0027t associate with trusts\n      self.post(\u0027/OS-TRUST/trusts\u0027, body\u003d{\u0027trust\u0027: ref},\n                expected_status\u003dhttp_client.BAD_REQUEST)\n\n  def test_create_trust_with_missing_attributes_and_extra_attributes_fails(self):\n      ref \u003d unit.new_trust_ref(trustor_user_id\u003dself.user_id,\n                               trustee_user_id\u003dself.trustee_user_id,\n                               project_id\u003dself.project_id)\n      ref.update({\u0027roles\u0027: {\u0027fake_key\u0027: \u0027fake_value\u0027}})\n\n      # Should return 403 Forbidden because `roles` is a required\n      # attribute\n      self.post(\u0027/OS-TRUST/trusts\u0027, body\u003d{\u0027trust\u0027: ref},\n                expected_status\u003dhttp_client.BAD_REQUEST)\n\n\nI would argue that both cases should return a 400 but apparently that\u0027s not the case the current trust API:\n\n  \u0027{\"error\": {\"title\": \"Forbidden\", \"message\": \"You are not authorized to perform the requested action: At least one valid role should be specified.\", \"code\": 403}}\u0027",
